# p1-reference -- reproduce Kostarelas's working configuration under KVM.
#
# TIER 2: deliberately references %VENDOR%. That is the point of P1 -- boot
# something known to have worked, so a failure is ours and not the
# firmware's. bin/tier-check.sh will flag this profile, which is correct.
# P3 replaces every one of these paths with something built from source.
#
# Provenance: docs/utm-bundle-config.md, the actual contents of
# Mavericks-OSX-10.9-Config.utm.zip, translated from UTM/TCG to plain
# QEMU/KVM. Where the bundle disagrees with the briefs, the bundle wins --
# it is the only verified 10.9-on-QEMU install we have.

@include base-kvm

# vmport=off comes straight from the bundle's MachineProperties.
-machine
q35,vmport=off

# Penryn plus exactly these three flags. The bundle has no popcnt, xsave,
# xsaveopt and no 'check' -- khronokernel's longer string from the briefs is
# NOT what actually worked. vendor is GenuineIntel already on this host.
-cpu
Penryn,+ssse3,+sse4.1,+sse4.2

# Firmware: the bundle's EDK II build, passed with -bios rather than as a
# pflash CODE/VARS pair.
#
# The bundle marks it ImageType "bios", which is UTM for -bios: a complete
# firmware image, not the CODE half of a split pair. The sizes agree --
# 1,966,080 for OVMF.bin and 540,672 for efi_vars.fd sum to no standard
# flash geometry -- and a first attempt wiring them as pflash unit 0/1 left
# the guest stuck at "Guest has not initialized the display (yet)".
#
# Consequence: EFI variables are not persisted between runs. That is what
# the reference configuration did, and it is fine for an installer boot.
-bios
%IMAGES%/work/OVMF_CODE.fd

# q35 provides no USB bus of its own, so the controller must be explicit --
# the first boot attempt failed with "No 'usb-bus' bus found". UTM adds one
# implicitly, which is why the bundle's config does not mention it.
#
# EHCI with UHCI companions, NOT qemu-xhci. With xhci, OpenCore booted from
# the USB image perfectly -- OVMF drives xhci fine -- but once Mavericks took
# over, neither keyboard nor mouse produced any input at all. 10.9's
# AppleUSBXHCI cannot drive QEMU's controller. This is the chipset layout a
# real Mac of the era has, and the drivers are correspondingly solid.
#
# All three UHCI functions are needed: EHCI only speaks high speed, and
# usb-kbd/usb-mouse are full-speed devices that get routed to a companion.
# masterbus ties them into one logical usb.0, so devices still just say
# bus=usb.0 and QEMU routes by speed.
-device
ich9-usb-ehci1,id=usb,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.7,multifunction=on
-device
ich9-usb-uhci1,masterbus=usb.0,firstport=0,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.0,multifunction=on
-device
ich9-usb-uhci2,masterbus=usb.0,firstport=2,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.1
-device
ich9-usb-uhci3,masterbus=usb.0,firstport=4,bus=pcie.0,addr=0x1d.2

# OpenCore over USB, as in the bundle. This image is byte-identical to
# khronokernel's published EFI-LEGACY.img, so we use his directly.
# It carries FakeSMC-32, VirtualSMC and Lilu -- which is why there is no
# -device isa-applesmc here and no OSK string anywhere.
# A DERIVED copy of khronokernel's image, kept INSIDE the quarantine under
# derived/. Deriving from a Tier 2 blob does not launder it: the result is
# still something we cannot rebuild from source, and putting it in work/
# made bin/tier-check.sh report clean while we still depended on it. The
# gate is only worth having if a copy cannot escape it.
#
# Not the original. The only change
# is flipping Kernel > Block > com.apple.driver.AppleTyMCEDriver from
# Enabled:false to true.
#
# The first boot to reach the kernel panicked in AppleTyMCEDriver's
# enableInterruptForCorrectableMemoryCoreRegister -- the Xeon machine-check
# driver, which loads because this config sets SMBIOS to MacPro5,1. It never
# fired for Kostarelas because he ran under TCG on Apple Silicon. khronokernel
# had already anticipated it: the block entry ships in the image, just
# disabled. So this enables a fix that was already written, rather than
# inventing one.
-drive
id=opencore,if=none,format=raw,file=%VENDOR%/derived/opencore-p1.img
-device
usb-storage,bus=usb.0,drive=opencore

# Installer as a CD on IDE. Kostarelas converted the dmg to an ISO in Disk
# Utility and attached it to the bundle's empty removable ide CD slot.
-drive
id=installer,if=none,format=raw,readonly=on,file=%IMAGES%/media/InstallMavericks.iso
-device
ide-cd,bus=ide.1,drive=installer

# Target disk on IDE, as in the bundle.
-drive
id=target,if=none,format=qcow2,file=%IMAGES%/work/mavericks.qcow2
-device
ide-hd,bus=ide.0,drive=target

# usb-net, not e1000-82545em. DarwinKVM recommends e1000 for 10.9 and the
# design took that as the starting point, but the configuration that is
# known to work uses usb-net. e1000 is now an experiment, not the baseline.
-netdev
user,id=net0
-device
usb-net,bus=usb.0,netdev=net0

# Input. usb-mouse, NOT usb-tablet.
#
# OS X cannot drive QEMU's usb-tablet natively -- that is exactly why
# pmj/QemuUSBTablet-OSX exists as a third-party kext, and Somlo's KVM guide
# specifies usb-kbd + usb-mouse. Wiring a tablet here produced a guest
# cursor pinned at the top-left that received no motion events at all.
#
# Absolute positioning (no pointer-grab, cursor follows the host) is a P5
# integration question, not something to fake with a device the guest has no
# driver for.
-device
usb-kbd,bus=usb.0
-device
usb-mouse,bus=usb.0

# DEVIATION FROM THE BUNDLE: it uses virtio-vga-gl, which wants a GL-capable
# local display. Starting with plain VGA so the guest can run headless and be
# screenshotted from the QEMU monitor. Kostarelas saw ~3MB of VRAM and could
# not change it under UTM; plain QEMU can, so find out early whether 10.9
# notices 64MB. Display device is a P5 experiment in its own right.
-device
VGA,vgamem_mb=64
